# Source: HoareLogic Exercises.pdf ("ICT3101 Exercises, Deductive Verification"), problem 3 a), with its solution in "HoareLogic Exercises Solutions - problems 1 - 3.pdf".
id: fill-precondition-true
title: Find the precondition of two assignments to y
topic: Sequential composition
level: 1
source: HoareLogic Exercises, problem 3 a
tags: assignment, sequence, wp

task:
  Find the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition $Q$.
  Calculate it statement by statement from the last one.

  The question only asks for the precondition. To have a complete triple,
  the exercise uses the precondition $P$: $true$, which places no
  condition on the initial state. At the end, the consequence rule checks
  this precondition.

pre: true
post: y = 3 * x

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  y := x;            // i1
  {R1}
  y := x + x + y     // i2
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of y := x + x + y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression ($x + x + y$) in
  the postcondition $Q$. The $y$ inside $x + x + y$ is the value of $y$
  before the assignment, so it stays as it is.
calc:
  wp(y := x + x + y, Q)
  = (y = 3 * x)[y <- x + x + y]
  = x + x + y = 3 * x      // the y of Q is replaced by x + x + y
  = y = x                  // subtract x + x from both sides
set: R1 = y = x

step: R2, the weakest precondition of y := x
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression
  ($x$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R1$.
calc:
  wp(y := x, R1)
  = (y = x)[y <- x]
  = x = x                  // the y of R1 is replaced by x
  = true                   // x = x holds for every x
set: R2 = true

step: P implies R2
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R2
text:
  $R2$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R2$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R2»
vc: true ==> true
why:
  Any formula implies $true$.
result: The weakest precondition is $true$. The program establishes $y = 3 * x$ from any initial state, and the triple is valid.

summary:
  The first assignment makes $y$ equal to $x$. The second one then gives
  $y$ the value $x + x + x$, which is $3 * x$. This is why no condition on
  the initial state is needed.
